### Description

An unauthenticated malformed UDP query to an XDNS listener can cause the entire Xray process to exit before client authentication. I reproduced the crash with the official v26.11.9 binary (`676eaf6`, Go 1.27.3) on darwin/arm64 through a loopback listener and on linux/arm64 over the public internet.

In `transport/internet/finalmask/xdns/server.go`, the type-3 length check allows decoded payloads as short as 13 bytes, but fragment data starts at byte offset 15. A decoded fragment of 13 or 14 bytes therefore panics when the parser slices the data at line 211, before `FragManager.Feed` runs. The log below shows the 13-byte case. The panic went unrecovered in the XDNS receive goroutine, and Xray exited with status 2. The same length check is present in `main` at `4d0bfb4d`.

A possible fix is to check the decoded length before reading the fragment header or slicing its data. The fragment branch needs at least 15 bytes for safe slicing, or 16 if the protocol requires a nonempty fragment. The existing minimum for non-fragment type-3 data should still apply. I have not tested a patch.

The length check was introduced in #6718 (`fc8f8a45`), first tagged in v26.9.30. #5657 addressed a different XDNS panic. I searched prior XDNS and slice-bounds reports and found no match for this case.

### Reproduction Method

I reproduced the panic on darwin/arm64 through the loopback UDP listener using the config below, then confirmed the same panic on linux/arm64 by sending one malformed query to the XDNS listener over the public internet. No Xray client or credentials were needed.

For a parser-level regression test, cover decoded type-3 fragments of 13 and 14 bytes in the `(*xdnsServer).read` path. Both should be rejected without a panic or any data delivered to the transport. Include a valid 13-byte non-fragment type-3 case and check that its data still reaches the transport.

